-- title:
--   Town balls grow with the radius
-- statement:
--   For real scale parameters $h$ (the town scale) and $\ell$ (the block size), a centre
--   $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and radii $R \le R'$, let
--
--   $$\mathrm{TB}(h,\ell;z,R) \;=\; \big\{\, Q_\ell(h\,p) \;:\; p \in \mathbb{Z}^d,\ \lVert p - z\rVert < R \,\big\}
--   \qquad (\texttt{townBall } h\ \ell\ z\ R)$$
--
--   denote the collection of blocks $Q_\ell(x) = \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \{y : \max_i|y_i - x_i| \le \ell/2\}$
--   (`block ℓ x`) whose lattice index $p$ lies within $R$ of $z$. Then
--
--   $$\mathrm{TB}(h,\ell;z,R) \;\subseteq\; \mathrm{TB}(h,\ell;z,R') .$$
--
--   Monotonicity in the radius is the bookkeeping lemma that lets a town ball computed at one radius be
--   reused at any larger one; combined with finiteness it gives the uniform vertex-count bounds the
--   path-length estimates rest on.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The scales $h$ and $\ell$ and the radii are arbitrary reals; the only
--   hypothesis is the inequality $R \le R'$, which is not required to be strict and does not require
--   either radius to be positive.
